Now I have the full picture. Let me produce the formal audit.

---

## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · **.NET 10** · **Integration tests** (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

All 19 smells from the testsmells.org academic catalog were evaluated. Integration-test calibration was applied: Mystery Guest, Eager Test, and Sleepy Test severities are adjusted for tests legitimately using real (in-memory) resources.

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High | 3 | 3 |
| Medium | 1 | 1 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **4** |

---

### Findings by Severity

---

#### ❶ Sleepy Test — **High**

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, method `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // blocks thread for 3 seconds
```

Even for integration tests, `Thread.Sleep` is a smell: the 3-second wait is arbitrary, non-deterministic, and thread-blocking in an `async Task` method. If the notification is faster, you waste time; if slower, the test flakes.

**Fix — use a polling/retry loop or async wait:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with timeout instead of fixed sleep
    var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
    var interval = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100);
    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
    while (sw.Elapsed < timeout)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
            break;
        await Task.Delay(interval);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        "Notification was not sent within the timeout period.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures, 3 s of wasted wall-clock time per run, thread-pool starvation from `Thread.Sleep` in an async method.

---

#### ❷ Conditional Test Logic — **High**

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, method `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101-108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

The `if`/`else` means only one assertion path ever executes. If the type hierarchy changes, the test silently falls through to the weaker branch and may pass incorrectly.

**Fix — write two deterministic tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    var user = new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m);
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com") as PremiumUser;

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Hidden testing gap — one branch is never covered; failures in the untested branch go undetected.

---

#### ❸ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — **High**

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, method `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113-119)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // No assertion — passes as long as no exception is thrown
}
```

The method name implies a "does not throw" intent, but MSTest will report this as a pass even if `Insert` silently drops every record. There is no verification that 100 rows were actually persisted.

**Fix — add a count assertion:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count, "All bulk-inserted users should be persisted.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — silent data loss or constraint violations would never be caught.

---

#### ❹ Assertion Roulette — **Medium**

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, method `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35-38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);          // which one failed?
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

Four assertions with no message parameters. When one fails in CI, the failure output shows only the assertion type and expected/actual values — no indication of *which* property was wrong without reading the code.

**Fix — add message parameters:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "Inserted user should be retrievable by email.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should match the inserted value.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should match the inserted value.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Persisted user should have a positive database ID.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower diagnosis in CI; developers must read test source to identify which property caused the failure.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The remaining **4 tests** are well-designed:

| Test | Why it's clean |
|------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, focused assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single concern, clean null-check verification |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count `3` is self-documenting (3 items just inserted); `CollectionAssert` is idiomatic |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Good concurrency stress test, correct `await`, clear assertion |

The `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` fixture is appropriately scoped — both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by every test (no **General Fixture** smell). Using in-memory SQLite is clean for integration tests (no **Mystery Guest** after calibration).

---

### Smells Not Detected (15 of 19)

Mystery Guest (calibrated out — in-memory DB), Eager Test, Magic Number Test, Sensitive Equality, Exception Handling, General Fixture, Ignored Test, Duplicate Assert, Lazy Test, Constructor Initialization, Default Test, Redundant Print, Redundant Assertion, Resource Optimism, Empty Test.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Impact |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** | Add count assertion to `BulkInsert` | Prevents false-pass; ~1 line |
| 2 | **Conditional Test Logic** | Split `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` into two tests | Eliminates hidden gap; ~10 min |
| 3 | **Sleepy Test** | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll loop | Fixes flakiness + speed; ~5 min |
| 4 | **Assertion Roulette** | Add message params to `InsertUser` assertions | Improves CI diagnostics; ~2 min |

Items 1–3 prevent **false-pass and flaky-failure** scenarios and should be addressed first. Item 4 is a maintainability improvement with lower urgency.
